          %h2#general_case General case
          %p
            On this page, we explain a connection between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                degroupoidification
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/degroupoidification"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids+internal"} groupoids internal
            to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+field"} finite field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
          %h3#basic_data Basic data
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\rightrightarrows Y
            be a groupoid internal to the category of schemes (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is the scheme of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the scheme of
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          %p Then we can make two constructions from this scheme:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "-points" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔽
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(\mathbb{F}_p) \rightrightarrows Y(\mathbb{F}_p)
                form a finite groupoid (with no additional structure).
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
                scheme
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(X\rightrightarrows Y)
                given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\rightrightarrows Y
                (this sends
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %msub
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Y
                        %mi X
                        %msub
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Y
                        %mi ⋯
                        %msub
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Y
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_Y X\times_Y\cdots  \times_Y X
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    One special case is when this is an
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi G
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                    (say) acting on
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Y
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Y
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y//G
                    (more properly
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ⫽
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \sslash G
                    = succeed "." do
                      %span.newWikiWord
                        GIT quotients
                        %a{:href => "/nlab/new/GIT+quotient"} ?
                %li
                  %p
                    More generally, but for this notational clash,
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y//X
                    could denote the nerve. Another option is
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi 𝒩
                            %mi Y
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}Y
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                    denotes the whole groupoid, rather than just the scheme of arrows.
          %h3#linearization_and_the_function_sheaf_correspondence Linearization, and the function sheaf correspondence
          %p
            The latter construction can be “linearized” in an analogous way to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
            %span.newWikiWord
              derived category of sheaves with finite rank constructible cohomology
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/constructible+derived+category"} ?
            with coefficients in the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+number"} adics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_\ell
            for some prime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(X\rightrightarrows Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(N(X\rightrightarrows Y))
            denote this category. In fact, we will require the graded version of this category, provided by
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                mixed sheaves
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/mixed+sheaf"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{mix}(N(X\rightrightarrows Y))
          %p
            There is a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{q,\ell}
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^0(D_{mix}(N(X\rightrightarrows Y))
            to the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_\ell[N(X \rightrightarrows Y)(\mathbb{F}_q)]
            of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_\ell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
            point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(X\rightrightarrows Y)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            is a prime and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo ∤
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell\nmid q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^n
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                        %mi ℤ
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔽
                          %mi q
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_{\mathrm{Spec}\mathbb{Z}}  \mathrm{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{F}_q}
          %p
            This map has the property that multiplying by the
            %span.newWikiWord
              motivic integral
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/motivic+integral"} ?
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            (i.e.,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_!m^*
            for the map
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:X\times \mathbb{A}^1\to X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
          %p
            Furthermore, no non-zero element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^0(D_{mix}(N(X\rightrightarrows Y))
            is killed by this map for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⇉
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi f
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⇉
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1)\overset{f}\leftarrow N(Z\rightrightarrows W) \overset{g}\rightarrow N(X_2 \rightrightarrows Y_2)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            of groupoids of schemes, then we have a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_!f^*:D_{mix}(N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1))\to D_{mix}(N(X_2 \rightrightarrows Y_2))
          %p
            Furthermore, there is an induced span of groupoids on this set of
            = succeed "-points," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi gf
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (gf)^\dagger
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi ℓ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi ℓ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_\ell[N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1)(\mathbb{F}_q)]\to \mathbb{Q}_\ell[N(X_2 \rightrightarrows Y_2)(\mathbb{F}_q)]
          %p
            The content of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+trace+formula"} Grothendieck trace formula
            connects these two constructions.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo !
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_!f^*:D_{mix}(N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1))\to D_{mix}(N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1))
              induces a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi gf
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo †
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi mix
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⇉
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (gf)_\dagger:K^0(D_{mix}(N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1)))\to K^0(D_{mix}(N(X_2 \rightrightarrows Y_2)))
              such that the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi K
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi D
                              %mi mix
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ⇉
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi gf
                                %msub
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo †
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi K
                              %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi D
                              %mi mix
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 2
                            %mo ⇉
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi q
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi ℓ
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi q
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi ℓ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi ℚ
                              %mi ℓ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ⇉
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝔽
                              %mi q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi gf
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo †
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi ℚ
                              %mi ℓ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 2
                            %mo ⇉
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝔽
                              %mi q
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K^0(D_{mix}(N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1))&amp;\overset{(gf)_\dagger}\to&amp; K^0(D_{mix}(N(X_2 \rightrightarrows Y_2)))   \\
                    \\downarrow^{\alpha_{q,\ell}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\alpha_{q,\ell}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{Q}_\ell[(N(X_1\rightrightarrows Y_1)\mathbb{F}_q)] &amp;\overset{(gf)^\dagger}\to&amp; \mathbb{Q}_\ell[N(X_2 \rightrightarrows Y_2)(\mathbb{F}_q)]
                    }
          %p That is, modulo a few details:
          .standout
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
              of the mixed derived category and
              %span.newWikiWord
                degroupoidification
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/degroupoidification"} ?
              of the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
              points coincide.
          %p
            We note that in many examples of geometric
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Since all groupoids appearing below are action groupoids, I’ll denote them
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/G
            in place of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times X\rightrightarrows X
          %h3#TheHeckeAlgebra The Hecke algebra
          %p If one takes the span of groupoids
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %mo \
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %mo \
                          %mi G
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %mo \
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %mo \
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{B\backslash G/B &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\
                  \&amp;\nwarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;\\
                  \&amp;&amp; B\backslash G\times_B G/B &amp;\to &amp;B\backslash G/B\\
                  \&amp;\swarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;\\
                  B\backslash G/B &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\}
          %p
            over the action groupoid for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\times B
            acting on the left and right on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            for a simple algebraic group, then the resulting monoidal category is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hecke+category"} Hecke category
            of G. Its decategorification is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hecke+algebra"} Hecke algebra
            of G, which is checked by showing the Hecke algebra is groupoidified by the F_q points of this, a result which was proved
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=165016"} Iwahori in 1964
          %h3#GeometricSatake Geometric Satake
          %p
            Consider the groupoid given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\mathbb{Z}[t])\times G(\mathbb{Z}[t])
            (for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[t]
            the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+ring"} polynomial ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\mathbb{Z}(t))
            considered as as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+schemes"} ind-schemes
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of this groupoid scheme is monoidal by the analogous diagram to that
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#TheHeckeAlgebra"} above
          %p
            The groupoidification of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
            points was shown by Satake (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Satake+equivalence"} Satake equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi L
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {^L G}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+dual+group"} Langlands dual group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo \
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{mix}(G(\mathbb{Z}[t])\backslash G(\mathbb{Z}(t)/ G(\mathbb{Z}[t])
            also contains a categorification of the representation ring of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi L
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {^L G}
          %p
            In fact, Mirković and Vilonen showed that the subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi mix
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo \
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{mix}(G(\mathbb{Z}[t])\backslash G(\mathbb{Z}(t))/ G(\mathbb{Z}[t])
            consisting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perverse+sheaves"} perverse sheaves
            is equivalent to the category of representations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mi L
                    %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                      %mi ℤ
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mi ℓ
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {^L G}\times_{\mathrm{Spec}\mathbb{Z}} \mathrm{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}_\ell}
            as an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+group"} algebraic group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_\ell
            by any other ring both in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
            of the sheaves, and the base of the algebraic group, the result still holds.
          %h3#the_hall_algebra_and_lusztigs_categorification The Hall algebra and Lusztig’s categorification
          %p
            If one takes the groupoid of representations of a Dynkin quiver with fixed dimension vector and basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_d
            (with each vector only having components over one dot), with the morphisms given by isomorphisms of representations (note, this is the action groupoid for a product of general linear groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL_d
            acting on a finite dimensional vector space), the resulting simplicial scheme is the fine moduli space of representations of that quiver.
          %p There is a span of groupoids
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mrow
                              %mi d
                              %mo ″
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi GL
                            %mrow
                              %mi d
                              %mo ″
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} SES
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi d
                          %mo ,
                          %mi d
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ,
                          %mi d
                          %mo ″
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi GL
                            %mi d
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mrow
                              %mi d
                              %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msub
                            %mi GL
                            %mrow
                              %mi d
                              %mo ′
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{E_{d''}/GL_{d''} &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\
                  \&amp;\nwarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathrm{SES}(d,d',d'')&amp;\to &amp;E_d/GL_d\\
                  \&amp;\swarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;\\
                  E_{d'}/GL_{d'} &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\\}
          %p
            where the span in the middle is the set of short exact sequences of representations with basis of dimension vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d',d''
            mapping into and out of one of dimension vector
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL_d
            preserving this filtration).
          %p
            It was proved by Ringel that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
            points of this variety groupoidify the upper half of the quantum group (of course, not in that language). The natural resulting categorification was described by Lusztig and used to construct the canonical basis of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_q(\mathfrak{g})
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is the simple Lie algebra associated to this quiver.
          %p This categorification was described independently by Rouquier and Khovanov–Lauda combinatorially.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satake_isomorphism"} Satake isomorphism
